WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:05.550
There's no shortage of mysteries to
be solved right now in cosmology.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:11.550
How did the universe begin? How is it
evolving? What is its ultimate fate?

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:17.162
I want to build instruments to explore
parts of the universe that are not seen yet.

00:00:17.162 --> 00:00:20.604
I hope that there are
surprises waiting for us.

00:00:22.648 --> 00:00:24.150
My name is Cynthia Chiang.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.940
I'm an associate professor in
physics at McGill University,

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.540
and I'm an observational cosmologist,
so I build telescopes for a living.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.880
One of the wonderful things about radio
astronomy is that we are able to see the

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:38.640
universe in ways that we
cannot see with our own eyes.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:43.530
If you pick a particular frequency lower than 1420 megahertz,

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.710
you can tune into a particular
slice of the universe's history.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:50.160
We think that the universe
began its life in the Big Bang,

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.200
which happened about 14 billion years ago.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.970
That time the universe was very hot and
very dense. As the universe has expanded

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.730
and it's cooled down, it's
transitioned through different phases.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.600
Hydrogen is a very special
tool for us in cosmology,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:07.590
and the reason why we rely on it so
heavily is because it's the most abundant

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:08.730
element in the universe,

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.630
and hydrogen is special because it
naturally glows with radio light,

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.740
and we can use this emission of hydrogen
to kind of map out where it is in the universe. 

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.610
Anytime you look at
any object with a telescope,

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.960
you're kind of seeing that object in the
past. Because the universe is expanding,

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:29.640
the light from the hydrogen is stretched
out or redshifted in proportion to how

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.450
far away the hydrogen is or equivalently
how long ago the light was emitted.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.040
So the way we can use radio telescopes
to kind of peer back in time and map out

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.940
the history of the universe is by
measuring this redshifted emission from

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:42.773
hydrogen.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.150
The first thing that you need for a radio
telescope is something to receive the

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:54.150
light, and so depending on what
kind of instrument you're building,

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:56.280
that can either be a reflector,

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.990
like a dish of some sort that gathers
light and then focuses it onto a receiver.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.250
Or sometimes in our experiments we just
have bare antennas that sit and look at

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:06.090
the sky. 

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:07.350
We have to bring it down about halfway.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.920
The radio signals that we're looking
for are typically very faint,

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.340
and so immediately after
you receive that radiation,

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.200
the first thing that you
want to do is amplify it,

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.840
And then there's usually digitizing
electronics that turn those analog signals

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:25.650
from the telescope into numbers that
then we can put on the computer. 

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.290
Start off in one sec. Alright, go ahead.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:34.710
The reward in doing this work is that
by knowing the science and the kinds of

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:35.730
things that you have to measure,

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.270
that informs our design so that
we're able to build a machine that's

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:40.770
specifically targeting our goals,

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.750
and then we can optimize
it to our heart's content.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.320
It's going to take off, climb to 50
feet, and then hover over the antenna.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:50.400
Then we'll run config F P G A and we'll
start up a dump base band dump spectra

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:51.330
and send it up to fly past.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.950
One of the greatest challenges of
observing in the radio is that there's all

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:04.038
sorts of interference at radio
wavelengths created by humans.

00:03:04.038 --> 00:03:08.380
FM radio, AM radio, TV towers, cell phone
towers, all sorts of stuff.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:13.420
So we often end up in strange places
out in the middle of nowhere where it's

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:17.110
quite literally quiet, radio quiet, so
that we can get a better view of the sky.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.460
The most recent sites that I've
worked at are in the high Arctic,

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:26.470
Northern Canada and also Marion Island,
which is an island in the sub Antarctic.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:29.710
It's located about halfway between
South Africa and Antarctica,

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:32.260
and it's about 2000 kilometers
away from anything else.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:35.920
Marion is windy and it's rainy
and it's cold year round.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:37.570
The winds are especially bad.

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:47.170
My team has two experiments that ran
from Marion Island and one of 'em,

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.840
PRIZM was designed to be
a Cosmic Dawn experiment,

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:53.380
so specifically looking for the signature
of the first stars illuminating in the

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:56.500
universe, roughly about a hundred
million years after the Big Bang.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:00.550
It's exciting to study
because we basically have no
measurements of this period.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:05.020
The experiment consists of two antennas
each about the size of a coffee table.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:10.060
The way the PRIZM antennas work is
that they are basically very sensitive

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.250
thermometers or microphones
as you can think about it.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:16.270
They just kind of measure the average
intensity across the entire sky.

00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:18.160
So we're not trying to make
pictures with these antennas.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:23.590
The frequencies that we're looking at
from Cosmic Dawn are actually exactly FM

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:25.750
radio that plus a little bit more.

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:30.190
So if you can imagine tuning into all
of the radio stations at the same time

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:31.150
with your car radio.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:34.360
What we're listening for is the
static from the early universe,

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:38.020
but within that static buried deep
within there for a few of those radio

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:41.260
stations we're listening for where
the static is a little quieter.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:50.860
The other experiment that we have set up
is an interferometer that is trying to

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:55.390
map out the low frequency sky as a first
step towards future observations of the

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.570
Dark Ages at about 400,000
years after the Big Bang.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:03.640
It's one of the periods in the most
distant history that we have yet to

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:07.600
access, and we have zero
measurements of the Dark Ages so far.

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:12.490
The universe turned from this glowing
ionized plasma and became transparent

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:16.390
Light was created at this point known
as the Cosmic Microwave Background,

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:19.060
and that's sometimes known as
the afterglow of the Big Bang.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:22.180
In addition to that light being released,

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:25.750
the universe became cold enough for the
first neutral hydrogen atoms to form.

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:29.410
The hydrogen that was created at that
point was basically all there was,

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:30.303
and the universe,

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:34.210
the Cosmic Microwave Background gives
us literally a photo of the universe in

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:35.043
its infancy.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:38.740
But that snapshot is also preserved in
the distribution of hydrogen throughout

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:39.573
the Dark Ages.

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:43.870
One of the very exciting things is
that if we could one day map out that

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:46.330
distribution of hydrogen
in full three dimensions,

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:50.980
we could learn a huge amount about our
universe. The ALBATROS experiment,

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:52.420
it's an array of antennas,

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.830
and these antennas will all observe
together to produce an image of the sky.

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:02.330
The next array that we're going to
build in full is in the high Arctic,

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:06.740
so when that's fully operational, the
antennas will be spanning about 10,

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:10.010
15 kilometers or so between the ones
that are most distant from each other.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:15.530
Our goal with ALBATROS is to make
maps or take pictures of our Milky Way

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:17.840
galaxy at very low frequencies,

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:20.840
and to get an image of the Milky
Way that's significantly sharper.

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:25.490
We have to understand our Milky Way
emission before we can subtract it off and

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:27.350
look for the Dark Ages signal behind it.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:31.190
So that's our very first step is kind
of knowing our enemy before we fight it.

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:33.620
Then we, we'll try to fight
it and get to the cosmology.

